  "rationale": "Direct-match candidates for rheumatoid arthritis are present (multiple models with trait_efo = rheumatoid arthritis). Among them, PGS004163 is best supported in the provided context because it has strong endpoint alignment (trait_reported/trait_efo: rheumatoid arthritis; phenotyping_reported: Seropositive RA) and comparatively strong, PRS-relevant validation performance evidence within a coherent same-publication cluster (\"Evaluation of polygenic scoring methods in five biobanks...\") with full-model AUROC 0.7469 and strong per-SD effect size (OR 2.46, with CI), plus a large evaluation sample (n=90,274) and no added covariates reported (covariates=\"0\"), reducing concern about covariate-driven inflation. However, the decision remains sub-optimal because many candidates report only full-model metrics (not PGS-only AUROC/R2), phenotype subtype specificity (seropositive RA) may not perfectly match a generic RA target, and there is meaningful competition from other direct-match RA models (e.g., PGS001311 with high full-model AUROC but very sparse 3-variant model and time-to-event endpoint; PGS002745 disease-focused Nat Genet RA score but with more modest AUROC and limited comparable PRS-only metrics)."
}

{"outcome":"DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL","best_model_id":"PGS005219","confidence":"Moderate","rationale":"Direct-match candidates are present for the target trait (sleep apnea), including multiple generic sleep-apnea scores (PGS003204–PGS003218) and obstructive sleep apnea (OSA) scores (PGS003479, PGS003857/PGS003858, PGS005219/PGS005220). Domain knowledge states OSA is the clinically dominant subtype of sleep apnea, so OSA-labeled models are acceptable direct matches and can be preferred when better supported.

Among the candidates, PGS005219 (Obstructive sleep apnea, AdjustedBMI) has the strongest visible PRS-strength evidence: it reports an OR=1.98 (95% CI 1.74–2.24) and full-model AUROC=0.79 in a multi-cohort training context (FinnGen, MGBB, MVP) with multi-ancestry evaluation listed, consistent with a disease-focused, multi-cohort PRS. Competing ExPRSweb ‘Sleep Apnea’ models (PGS003204–PGS003218) show near-null full-model AUROCs (~0.49–0.53) and weak/near-null effect sizes (ORs ~1.00), providing little support for meaningful PRS discrimination. PGS003857/PGS003858 report similar AUROC=0.77 but are genome-wide-significant small-variant scores (18 and 6 variants) and their strong AUROC is a full-model metric under BMI-adjusted phenotyping; without PGS-only metrics, that AUROC advantage is not fully comparable.

Main limitation: all top candidates (including PGS005219) lack PRS-only (covariate-free) AUC/R2, and BMI adjustment (both in phenotyping and as a covariate) complicates comparability to generic ‘sleep apnea’ risk; therefore the selection is best-supported but not high-certainty."}

{"outcome":"DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL","best_model_id":"PGS000613","confidence":"Moderate","rationale":"All 20 candidates are direct matches to urinary bladder cancer (trait_reported bladder cancer; trait_efo urinary bladder carcinoma/cancer; phenotyping_reported bladder cancer/incident bladder cancer/cancer of bladder/urothelial carcinoma). However, across the pool there are no clearly reported PRS-only (covariate-free) AUROC/R² metrics, so ranking relies on endpoint fidelity plus available effect-size/full-model metrics and structural signals, which limits certainty.

PGS000613 is preferred because it has strong endpoint alignment ('Cancer of bladder'), is part of the coherent Cancer PRSweb bladder-cancer cluster with comparable basic covariates (age, sex, batch, PCs 1–4), and shows among the strongest visible discriminative evidence within that cluster: full-model AUROC 0.571 with a relatively strong per-SD effect size (OR 1.301, 95% CI 1.227–1.379) and strong tail separation (OR top 1% vs rest 2.91, 95% CI 1.99–4.24). It also benefits from a larger development/training sample (n=12,992; UKB) than several near-identical siblings (e.g., n=7,469; MGI) while keeping the same endpoint family.

Main limitation: the best-looking full-model AUROCs in the set (e.g., PGS000071/PGS000782 ~0.80) are not PRS-only and are likely boosted by non-genetic covariates (notably smoking variables and BMI), making them non-comparable as evidence of PRS strength; meanwhile, several other candidates (e.g., PGS000611) have similar AUROC/OR evidence, so the superiority of PGS000613 is not decisive from the visible metadata alone."}

{
  "outcome": "DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL",
  "best_model_id": "PGS000611",
  "confidence": "Moderate",
  "rationale": "All listed candidates are direct matches to urinary bladder cancer (mostly trait_efo=urinary bladder carcinoma and phenotyping_reported explicitly bladder cancer / cancer of bladder), so a direct-match option exists. Among the candidates with PRS-relevant evidence not dominated by covariate-heavy full-model AUROC, the Cancer PRSweb UKB-derived GWAS-hits score PGS000611 shows comparatively strong signal in the visible metrics: full-model AUROC=0.567 with basic covariates (age, sex, batch, PCs1-4) and a notable top-1%-vs-rest OR=2.66 (1.79–3.93), alongside a per-unit OR=1.284 (1.211–1.361), outperforming closely related PRSweb siblings (e.g., PGS000610/612/613) and the shrinkage models whose AUROC is near ~0.52–0.54. However, confidence is limited because no candidate reports PRS-only AUROC or PRS-only R², making cross-model comparisons partly non-comparable; some alternatives (e.g., PGS000071/PGS000782) report very high full-model AUROC (~0.80) but include additional epidemiologic covariates (smoking/BMI) and lack incremental/PRS-only metrics, so their apparent advantage cannot be safely attributed to the PRS itself."
}

{"outcome":"DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL","best_model_id":"PGS002033","confidence":"Moderate","rationale":"All 10 candidates are direct matches to the target trait (obesity) by trait_efo, but endpoint fidelity and comparable performance reporting vary substantially. Several models have limited or non-comparable performance metadata (many lack PRS-only AUROC/R²; some report only a single OR/beta without clear per-SD definition), and one is a pan-trait sparse framework score (PGS001298) with modest PRS-only metrics and a time-to-event phenotype, which the provided domain guidance flags as often suboptimal versus more endpoint-faithful alternatives.

PGS002033 is selected because it offers a strong direct-match endpoint label ("Overweight, obesity and other hyperalimentation" with obesity in trait_efo) and provides the most informative available PRS-comparable performance signal among the portability-study models: a higher reported partial correlation (partial-r=0.0789) than its close sibling PGS001825 (partial-r=0.0547), with similar training/validation context (UKB-derived, EUR development; European selected validation ancestry). Competing candidates either (a) lack comparable performance metrics entirely (e.g., PGS004447/PGS004517/PGS005145/PGS005154/PGS005235), (b) have unclear phenotype alignment signals (e.g., PGS003400 lists phenotyping_reported as BMI despite trait_reported obesity), or (c) come from a lifestyle-exposure-focused context (PGS003959) with heavy covariate adjustment including baseline adiposity measures, which reduces comparability for a standalone obesity PRS.

Main limitation: no clear PRS-only AUROC/R² is reported for PGS002033, so selection rests on partial-r plus endpoint alignment and study context, leaving uncertainty about standalone discriminative performance and external generalizability relative to other candidates with missing/unclear metrics."}

{"outcome":"DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL","best_model_id":"PGS005262","confidence":"Moderate","rationale":"Direct-match candidates are present for nodular goiter, including (i) nontoxic multinodular goiter (PGS001814, PGS002022) and (ii) benign nodular goiter (PGS005261, PGS005262, PGS005263). Two additional models (PGS005273, PGS005274) are case-control contrasts of thyroid carcinoma vs benign nodular goiter and are less endpoint-faithful to a generic nodular goiter target because the phenotype includes carcinoma.

Among the most endpoint-aligned set (benign nodular goiter), PGS005262 is best supported by the visible performance evidence: it has the highest reported AUROC (0.5933) among the benign nodular goiter scores and also reports a stronger per-SD effect size signal (OR 1.408) than the sibling benign models. It also uses a genome-wide continuous shrinkage method (PRS-CS) with a very large variant count (~1.09M), which is structurally favorable.

However, the overall direct-match recommendation remains sub-optimal because none of the candidates report clearly PRS-only (covariate-free) discrimination (the AUROC shown is a full-model metric and covariates are listed as Unknown for the benign-nodular-goiter models), making cross-model comparability and standalone PRS strength uncertain."}

{
  "outcome": "DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL",
  "best_model_id": "PGS001280",
  "confidence": "Moderate",
  "rationale": "All 7 candidates are direct matches at the disease-concept level (pulmonary embolism appears in trait_efo and/or phenotyping_reported). The strongest endpoint-fidelity to the target trait without adding DVT is among the PE-only models (PGS001280, PGS003861, PGS004460, PGS004530). Among these, PGS001280 provides the most PRS-comparable evidence: it reports PGS-only AUROC (0.60765) and PGS R2 (no covariates) (0.01403), along with basic covariates (age, sex, PCs, array), and its PRS-only R2 is plausibly below the provided heritability ceiling sanity context. Competing PE-only candidates have limitations in the visible metadata: PGS003861 reports only a full-model AUROC (0.765) with unknown covariates (not PRS-comparable), and PGS004460/PGS004530 report only an OR without PRS-only discrimination metrics and also have unknown covariates. Remaining limitation: PGS001280 is time-to-event (TTE PE), which domain guidance flags as a potential endpoint mismatch for generic disease risk, and the evidence is drawn from a pan-trait UKB framework paper (snpnet across 813 traits), lowering certainty relative to a disease-focused PE study with clean PRS-only metrics (not present here)."
}
